# Written and kept up to date by bin/bootstrap — this file documents the keys.

# The host side of the /workspace bind. A sibling of the repo, never inside it.
FACTOR_IA_WORKSPACE_HOST=../factor-ia-workspace

# The container runs as this uid/gid, or bind mounts are unwritable and git
# refuses /workspace as dubiously owned. bin/bootstrap fills them from `id`.
FACTOR_IA_UID=1000
FACTOR_IA_GID=1000

# Optional. Authorship on the commits a run produces. Leave unset rather than
# empty — an empty git identity is worse than an absent one.
# GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=
# GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=
# GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=
# GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=

# Optional. Unset means UTC.
# TZ=Europe/Lisbon

# Extra origins allowed to send mutating requests (comma-separated). Unset for
# the compose stack; **required** for the Vite dev loop, where the page stays on
# :5173 and Vite's proxy rewrites Host to the backend, so a save would otherwise
# be refused as cross-origin.
# Mutating requests are accepted from the server's own origin or from no origin
# at all (curl); anything else is refused, so another page in your browser
# cannot drive the local server.
# FACTOR_IA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173

# Extra names the server answers to (comma-separated). localhost, 127.0.0.1 and
# [::1] are always answered, on any port; every other Host is refused, so a page
# that points its own name at your loopback cannot reach the API. A bare name
# allows every port, `name:port` that port only. Unset unless you reach the
# server by some other name.
# FACTOR_IA_ALLOWED_HOSTS=my-box.lan:8000

# Which agent adapter a run uses. Unset means the real `claude` CLI. `fake`
# drives the whole worktree/commit path on scripted, zero-token edits — the
# setting the test suite uses, and the one to reach for when debugging a
# factory rather than paying for it. An agent node can also name it per node
# with `"adapter": "fake"` in its config.
# FACTOR_IA_ADAPTER=fake

# The server-side opt-in DESIGN.md §6 requires before an agent node may select
# a `bypassPermissions`-shaped permission mode. Without it those modes are
# refused as a run precondition. When active, the run is marked dangerous and
# the canvas says so. Default policy without it: `acceptEdits` — file edits
# inside the run's own worktree are pre-approved, commands still ask, and an
# ask with nobody watching is a denial rather than a hung node.
# FACTOR_IA_DANGEROUS_PERMISSIONS=1
